=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Tetrad Analysis for Linked Genes in Unordered Tetrads*

@body

- Four common situations\:
  - No cross-overs or 2-strand double cross-overs result in [parental ditype (PD)]{color=#996600} asci.
  - One recombination between the genes results in [tetratype (TT)]{color=#8D1D75} asci.
  - Three-strand double cross-overs give the same result as a single crossover, [tetratype (TT)]{color=#8D1D75} asci.
  - Four-strand double cross-overs give [non-parental ditype (NPD)]{color=#009933} asci.
- As a result, for linked genes,
  - [PD]{color=#B47804} >> [NPD]{color=#00A933}.

=== layout: stacked-panels
@replaceme


@top

- NCO, noncrossover meioses; SCO, single-crossover meioses; DCO, double-crossover meioses

@bottom

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect09b-tetrads_1_introduction/image_020.png)

- Triples and higher numbers of crossovers might occur, but we may assume that such crossovers are rare and therefore negligible.

- NCO

- SCO

- DCO

- DCO

- DCO

- DCO

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- The key to the analysis is the [NPD]{color=#00A933} class
  - arises only from a double crossover between all four chromatids.
  - Because we are assuming that double crossovers occur randomly between the chromatids, we can also assume that the frequencies of the four DCO classes are equal.
- This assumption means that the [NPD]{color=#00A933} class should contain &#188; of the DCOs, and therefore we can estimate that

$$
DCO = 4 \cdot NPD
$$

@citation

In this derivation, class sizes are proportions of all tetrads.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- The single-crossover class also can be calculated by a similar kind of reasoning.
  - Notice that [tetratype (TT)]{color=#A1467E} asci can result from either single-crossover or double-crossover meioses.
  - But we can estimate the component of the [TT]{color=#A1467E} class that comes from DCO meioses to be 2&#215;[NPD]{color=#00A933}.
- Hence, the size of the SCO class can be stated as

$$
SCO = TT - 2 \cdot NPD
$$

@citation

In this derivation, class sizes are proportions of all tetrads.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Now that we have estimated the sizes of the SCO and DCO classes, the non-crossover class can be estimated as
$$
NCO = 1 - (SCO + DCO)
$$

- Thus, we have estimates of the sizes of the NCO, SCO, and DCO classes in this marked region.





$$
DCO = 4 \cdot NPD
$$

$$
SCO = TT - 2 \cdot NPD
$$

@citation

In this derivation, class sizes are proportions of all tetrads.

=== layout: one-panel

# From Crossovers to Map Distance

@body

- Let m be the mean number of crossovers per meiosis in this region.

$$
m = SCO + 2DCO = TT + 6NPD
$$

- Each crossover involves two of the four chromatids.
- Multiply m by 50 to express the map estimate in cM.

$$
D = 50m = \frac{1}{2}(TT + 6NPD) \times 100 \;\mathrm{cM}
$$

@citation

In this derivation, class sizes are proportions of all tetrads.

